(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ocular-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Advanced Ocular History & Examination Quiz 1 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why may a zygomatic fracture cause pain on opening the mouth?","choices":["The temporomandibular joint is always involved","The temporalis muscle passes medial to the arch and inserts on the mandible","The masseter originates on the mandible and inserts on the zygoma","The infraorbital nerve is compressed during jaw movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the anatomical explanation Beck gave alongside the finding. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Injection localised JUST AROUND THE CORNEA suggests which three conditions?","choices":["Subconjunctival haemorrhage, pinguecula, or pterygium","Episcleritis, chalazion, or hordeolum","Keratitis, iritis, or acute glaucoma","Conjunctivitis, blepharitis, or dry eye"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — these three, and this is the pattern that must not be dismissed as conjunctivitis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is interrupted in Horner syndrome?","choices":["The sympathetic innervation of the pupil and the levator palpebrae superioris","The parasympathetic supply travelling through the ciliary ganglion","The oculomotor nerve","The optic nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which explains both the small pupil and the ptosis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What is the defining behaviour of the Argyll Robertson pupil?","choices":["It reacts to both, but slowly","It accommodates but does not react to light","It reacts to light but does not accommodate","It reacts to neither light nor accommodation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it accommodates but does not react to light. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Eye pain with PHOTOPHOBIA suggests what?","choices":["Inflammation of the iris","Corneal abrasion","Temporal arteritis","Conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — photophobia points to iris inflammation. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which nerve mediates the pupillary constriction of the near reaction, and which two things accompany it?","choices":["The abducens nerve; accompanied by abduction","The oculomotor nerve; accompanied by convergence and accommodation","The optic nerve; accompanied by convergence and dilation","The trochlear nerve; accompanied by depression and intorsion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — convergence and accommodation are coincident with the constriction but not part of it. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Acute, unilateral and PAINLESS visual loss suggests which group of causes?","choices":["Simple glaucoma or cataract","Retinal vascular occlusion, retinal detachment, vitreous haemorrhage, macular degeneration","Corneal abrasion or ulcer, uveitis, traumatic hyphaema, acute narrow angle glaucoma","Thermal, radiation or chemical exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — these are the painless causes. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which compensatory finding accompanies diplopia?","choices":["A compensatory head posture","A compensatory squint of one eye only","Compensatory pupil dilation","Compensatory tearing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — patients tilt or turn to fuse the images. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is nystagmus, and when is a few beats normal?","choices":["Misalignment of the visual axes — a few degrees of deviation is normal","Drooping of the upper lid — a few millimetres is normal","Unequal pupil size — a few millimetres is normal","Fine rhythmic oscillation of the eyes — a few beats on LATERAL gaze is normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is the definition, and the normal exception. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, what is the CORNEA like in glaucoma?","choices":["Clear","Clear or slightly clouded","Changes depending on the cause","Steamy and cloudy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the chart's glaucoma column. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"What additional finding appears in CONGENITAL Horner syndrome?","choices":["Proptosis of the affected eye","The involved iris is lighter in colour — heterochromia","The involved iris is darker in colour than its fellow","Bilateral ptosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — heterochromia appears in the congenital form. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which complaints belong in the EMERGENT group, referred to ophthalmology immediately?","choices":["Blepharitis, chalazion and dry eye","Conjunctivitis and subconjunctival haemorrhage","Sudden vision loss, retinal artery occlusion, chemical burns, rupture, acute angle-closure glaucoma, and vitreous haemorrhage","Acute glaucoma, orbital cellulitis, corneal ulcer or abrasion, retinal detachment, macular oedema, and hyphaema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — these make up the emergent list. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Lateral sparseness of the eyebrows suggests what?","choices":["Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Iron deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lateral eyebrow loss points to hypothyroidism. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What happens when a mydriatic is instilled in an Argyll Robertson pupil?","choices":["Incomplete dilation","Full and rapid dilation","No change at all","Paradoxical constriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — mydriatics dilate it only incompletely. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"When must the lid never be everted?","choices":["If rupture of the globe is suspected","If the patient has conjunctivitis","If the patient wears contact lenses","If a foreign body has already been seen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — this is absolute. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, what is the PUPIL like in glaucoma?","choices":["Dilated and fixed","Not affected","May be small and, with time, irregular","Not affected unless iritis develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the chart's glaucoma column, and a cardinal danger sign. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Which corneal ulcer is NOT very painful?","choices":["Bacterial corneal ulcers","Fungal corneal ulcers","Amoebic corneal ulcers","Herpes simplex corneal ulcers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — herpes simplex is the exception. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Watery or mucoid discharge suggests which conditions?","choices":["Traumatic","Allergic or viral","Bacterial infection","Fungal infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — watery and mucoid discharge points to allergic or viral causes. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"When must pupillary reactions be checked, relative to dilation?","choices":["After dilating","It makes no difference","Only after the fundoscopic examination","Before dilating"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pupillary reactions must be checked before dilating. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What must be documented when the pupils are dilated?","choices":["The patient's consent form number","Nothing specific","The time of dilation and the agents used","The visual acuity before dilation only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both the time and the agents are recorded. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Eye pain with a GRITTY feeling suggests what?","choices":["Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Conjunctivitis","Inflammation of the iris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — grittiness goes with conjunctivitis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Bilateral visual loss usually implies which kind of problem?","choices":["A primary neurologic cause rather than a primary ophthalmologic one","A primary ophthalmologic cause rather than a neurologic one","An infective cause","A traumatic cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bilateral loss points neurologic rather than ophthalmologic. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What is trichiasis?","choices":["Outward turning of the lower lid margin","Drooping of the upper lid","Posteriorly misdirected eyelashes","Inward turning of the lower lid margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — posteriorly misdirected lashes, alongside entropion and ectropion. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"When should the lacrimal sac compression test be avoided?","choices":["If the patient has previously had cataract surgery on that eye","If the patient is over sixty-five","If the area is significantly inflamed or tender","If the patient wears contact lenses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — avoid it when the area is significantly inflamed or tender. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, what does it give as the significance of the glaucoma column?","choices":["Bacterial, viral and other infections, or allergy and irritation","Abrasions and other injuries; viral and bacterial infections","Often none; may result from trauma, bleeding disorders, or a sudden increase in venous pressure","An acute increase in intraocular pressure — an emergency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the chart labels it an emergency outright. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"When is globe rupture more likely in a patient with a subconjunctival haemorrhage?","choices":["When both eyes are affected","In trauma, and when the haemorrhage encircles the entire cornea","When the haemorrhage is small, flat and confined to one sector","When the patient is anticoagulated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — trauma plus a 360-degree haemorrhage. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"In a trauma patient, what must NOT be done?","choices":["Do not take a history","Do not palpate the globe","Do not measure visual acuity","Do not inspect the lids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — palpating the globe is the critical thing to avoid in trauma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which two conditions are associated with blepharitis?","choices":["Hypothyroidism and iron deficiency","Graves disease and myasthenia gravis","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus","Bacterial infection and atopic dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bacterial infection and atopic dermatitis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"After a chemical or fluid splash to the eye, what must be established?","choices":["The volume of fluid involved","Whether the patient was wearing contact lenses","The manufacturer of the product","The acidity or alkalinity of the fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — knowing whether it was acid or alkali is essential. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which presentation belongs in the EMERGENT referral group rather than the urgent one?","choices":["A chalazion that has persisted for two months","Gradual painless vision loss over a year","Sudden vision loss after a chemical splash","Itchy, watery red eyes every spring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a chemical splash with vision loss goes to ophthalmology immediately. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"}]};
